    <bf:title>
      <bf:Title>
	<bf:mainTitle>Enduring nakba : Palestinians after the war in Syria</bf:mainTitle>
      </bf:Title>
    </bf:title>
    <bf:content>
      <bf:Content rdf:about="http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/contentTypes/txt">
	<rdfs:label>text</rdfs:label>
	<bf:code>txt</bf:code>
      </bf:Content>
    </bf:content>
    <bf:tableOfContents>
      <bf:TableOfContents>
	<rdfs:label>Introduction -- "It's so hard to stay neutral in Syria" : Palestinians are drawn into the war -- War and siege : the shortcomings of institutionalized humanitarianism -- Survival under siege : Palestinians as providers of aid, education, and protection -- Secondary exile in the Middle East : Lebanon -- The crossing : seeking protection in Europe -- Palestinian refugees and Germany's welcome culture -- Encampment and the limits of Germany's welcome culture -- Palestinians from Syria and the French asylum visa -- Conclusion : identity, politics, and global (Im)mobility.</rdfs:label>
      </bf:TableOfContents>
    </bf:tableOfContents>
    <bf:summary>
      <bf:Summary>
	<rdfs:label>"One outcome of the onset of civil war in Syria was the mass displacement of Palestinian refugees after 2011. Existing Palestinian refugee camps were decimated and more than one hundred thousand people fled the country, many of whom ended up in Europe. This was no coincidence: Displacement of Palestinians from Syria was deliberately encouraged by the Syrian government, Israel, and Western powers united in a plan to end the Palestinian refugee question once and for all. Centering on the narratives of Palestinians affected by the war in Syria, Enduring Nakba explores the impact of recurrent, compounded, and expanded forced displacement on Palestinian identity and political claims. Focusing on the efforts of refugees to find protection and stability in the aftermath of war, Nell Gabiam offers a crucial reflection on the global politics of citizenship, noncitizenship, and (im)mobility"-- Provided by publisher.</rdfs:label>
      </bf:Summary>
    </bf:summary>
    <dcterms:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://id.loc.gov/resources/works"/>
